  {"question": "According to arXiv 2402.17861, what are two major challenges auditors face regarding data access and legal risks, and how do these relate to platform APIs and the CFAA?", "answer": "Auditors face challenges from platform-controlled APIs becoming costly or restricted, and legal risks under the CFAA for unauthorized data collection.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "There ' s a direct impact on shutting off the access to information that affects people doing audits... we saw that with Reddit charging for their API and shutting down... Twitter charging astronomical, now, amounts for their API."}, {"page": 10, "quote": "Some external auditors also worried that external data collection tools-particularly tools for data scraping or data donation-may violate terms of service set by platforms and result in legal liability or retaliation. Auditors expressed concerns about legal risks from existing laws and regulations, particularly the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA) and other privacy laws."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2402.17861, how do open source tools for Data Collection compare to Transparency Infrastructure tools in terms of availability and organizational origin?", "answer": "Data Collection tools are more likely to be open source and less often built by for-profit organizations than Transparency Infrastructure tools.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "Unlike the tools we found for Transparency Infrastructure, which were mostly not open source (25.0% open source in our dataset; see Fig. 3), tools for Data Collection were much more likely to be available under an open source license (68.1% open source in our dataset)."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2402.17861, what is the definition of 'accountability' as used by Costanza-Chock et al. and why is it considered a top unmet need?", "answer": "Accountability is defined as a 'commitment from auditee to address problems covered by audit within set time', and is a top unmet need because auditors struggle to achieve it.", "evidence": [{"page": 12, "quote": "Costanza-Chock et al. [136] found that over 65% of surveyed AI audit practitioners felt that 'accountability' (defined as a 'commitment from auditee to address problems covered by audit within set time') was a top unmet need in their AI auditing work."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2402.17861, what is the main concern with relying on performance analysis tools built by for-profit organizations?", "answer": "There is skepticism about the reliability and openness of such tools, as they may not be open source and can give undue power to their creators.", "evidence": [{"page": 13, "quote": "even the free tools currently dominating the audit tooling landscape were often built by large, for-profit tech companies (Fig. 2)-for example, 9 of the 12 Transparency Infrastructure tools we found were built by for-profit organizations. Audit tools like these can hold great power over the audit process:"}]} ,
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2402.17861, what is a key reason auditors prefer collecting data independently rather than relying on internal access?", "answer": "Independent data collection can be more revealing, as inside access may limit the auditor's perspective to what the organization allows.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "There ' s a lot of requests for inside access, as if that' s the only way to do this type of thing, but in reality inside access can actually be a trap... your view inside the room is actually really limited. And so I have come to believe... that actually doing analysis from the outside can often be way more revealing..."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2402.17861, what is the main obstacle auditors face when trying to combine and interpret large datasets during audits?", "answer": "Auditors spend most of their time understanding tables, columns, and determining meaningful metrics, making data management the most labor-intensive task.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "90% of the work is figuring out what different tables are, and what different columns are, and working out whether it makes sense to join certain things. And then figuring out some meaningful metrics that we can draw from that data..."}]}
]}
